==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{der|la|itc-pro|*auti}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₂ewti|t=on the other hand}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*h₂ew}}. Cognate with {{m|la|autem}}, {{cog|grc|αὖ}}, {{m|grc|αὖτε}}, {{m|grc|αὐτός}}, {{m|grc|αὐτάρ}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA}}

===Conjunction===
{{head|la|conjunction}}

# [[or]] ([[exclusive or]])
#* {{ux|la|'''Aut''' Caesar '''aut''' nihil.|All or nothing|lit='''Either''' a Caesar '''or''' a nothing.}}
#* {{ux|la|'''Aut''' disce '''aut''' discēde.|'''Either''' you learn, '''or''' go away.}}
#* {{Q|la|Vulgate|Tobiae|3|19|quote={{...}}et '''aut''' ego indigna fuī illīs '''aut''' illī mihi forsitan dignī nōn fuērunt.|t={{...}}and '''either''' I was unworthy for them, '''or''' they perhaps were not worthy for me.}}
# [[otherwise]], [[or else]] {{gl|a consequence of the condition that the previous is false}}
#: {{ux|la|Accipe nummōs nōnāgintā — '''aut''' nūllōs!|Take 90 sesterces — '''or''' none at all!}}
# {{n-g|Introduces a correction to the previous words or an afterthought remark.}}

====Usage notes====
* In the meaning (1) typically placed before each coordinated element (''[[aut]]''...''[[aut]]''...''[[aut]]''), equivalent to "{{m|en|either}}...{{m|en|or}}".
* Unlike {{m|la|vel}}, this word implies an exclusive "or"; i.e., one option or the other, but not both.

