==Latvian==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|lv|ine-bsl-pro|*áutei}}, from {{der|lv|ine-pro|*h₃ew-}}. Cognates include {{cog|lt|auti|aũti}}, {{cog|sla-pro|*uti||to put on}} (> {{m|sla-pro|*jьzuti|}}, {{m|sla-pro|*obuti}}), {{cog|hit|tr=unu-|t=to adorn, decorate, lay (the table)}}, {{cog|la|*uō||to put on}} (> {{m|la|exuō}}, {{m|la|induō}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{lv-IPA|àwt|falling}}
* Audio (LV): {{audio|lv|lv-riga-aut.ogg}}

===Verb===
{{lv-verb|tr|1st|aun|aun||āv|auj|auj}}

# [[put on]] [[footwear]] ([[shoe]]s, [[boot]]s, [[sock]]s, etc.)
#: {{ux|lv|zēns '''āva''' kājas|the boy '''put on''' footwear (lit. on his feet)|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|'''aut''' kājas pastalās|to '''put on''' pastalas (simple footwear) (lit. to put one's feet into pastalas)|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|'''aut''' kurpes kājas|to '''put on''' shoes (lit. to put shoes on one's feet)|inline=1}}
# ''nosēdos uz akmens un gribēju '''aut''' kājas, bet kurpes bija ļoti sabristas'' — I sat down on a rock and wanted to '''put shoes on''' (lit. to '''put''' (my) feet (into shoes)), but the shoes were very wet
#: {{ux|lv|Žanis '''āva''' kājās stulmeņu zābakus|Žanis '''put''' the long boots '''on''' (his) feet|inline=1}}
# {{lb|lv|figuratively|with {{l|lv|kājas}}}} to [[prepare]] for a [[journey]] (lit. to '''put on footwear''')
#: {{ux|lv|un tūliņ ķēniņš '''aun kājas''' savu sievu meklēt|and quickly the king '''puts on footwear''' to go looking for his wife|inline=1}}

====Usage notes====
Note that {{l|lv|aut}} can take two complements, the footwear or the subject's feet. Either can be the direct object, in which case the other will be a locative complement (i.e., either "to put shoes on one's feet" or "to put one's feet into shoes").

